  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaOH</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mi>OH</mi></math> is alcoholic<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>NaOH</mi></math> which is used for dehydrohalogenation of alkenes. This beta elimination&nbsp;is anti-elimination and Saytzeff alkene (more stable) is major product. In the given reaction, more stable conjugated alkene is formed as major product.</p><p><img alt=\"\" src=\"assets\\st0072_ba6e53dee740.png\" style=\"width: 462px; height: 155px;\" />Note : There is ambiguity in answer given by NTA&nbsp;which is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>"
